1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,209
Currying, closures, and I I F Es. Some version of

2
00:00:03,309 --> 00:00:06,519
these shows up on every single exam, usually as a

3
00:00:06,619 --> 00:00:11,180
short rewrite question. They're quick points once the pattern clicks.

4
00:00:13,266 --> 00:00:17,678
Here's a normal function of three arguments: a times b minus c.

5
00:00:17,766 --> 00:00:22,762
Currying means splitting those arguments across separate calls. For the shape

6
00:00:22,862 --> 00:00:26,005
a comma b, then c, the outer function takes a and

7
00:00:26,105 --> 00:00:28,850
b, and returns a new function that takes c.

8
00:00:28,933 --> 00:00:32,201
The trick: count the sets of parentheses in the call.

9
00:00:32,301 --> 00:00:35,505
Each set is one level of function in the definition.

10
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:39,634
For the shape a, then b comma c, the arrow version is

11
00:00:39,734 --> 00:00:44,236
very compact. a arrow, b comma c arrow, and the expression.

12
00:00:44,333 --> 00:00:47,809
Let's call all three with 2, 5, and 3.

13
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,108
All three give 7. Same math, different call shapes.

14
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:00,888
Why does currying work? Closures. When getValueCurried returns the inner function,

15
00:01:00,988 --> 00:01:04,842
that inner function keeps a and b alive, even though the

16
00:01:04,942 --> 00:01:08,372
outer call is over. So tenMinus remembers 2 and 5.

17
00:01:08,466 --> 00:01:11,779
A classic closure example: makeCounter keeps a private

18
00:01:11,879 --> 00:01:14,750
count, and returns an arrow that increments it.

19
00:01:14,833 --> 00:01:17,877
We make two counters, and call them in a mixed order.

20
00:01:18,266 --> 00:01:24,166
tenMinus gives 9 and 6. And the counters print 1, 2, 1, 3.

21
00:01:25,266 --> 00:01:29,384
Exam trap. c1 and c2 do not share a count. Every call to

22
00:01:29,484 --> 00:01:34,430
makeCounter creates a fresh count variable, so c2 starts over at 1.

23
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:41,124
An I I F E is an immediately invoked function expression.

24
00:01:41,224 --> 00:01:44,521
The exam gives you a named function and a call, like

25
00:01:44,621 --> 00:01:47,984
this, and asks you to rewrite them as one expression.

26
00:01:48,066 --> 00:01:52,568
Wrap the function in parentheses, then call it right away with another

27
00:01:52,668 --> 00:01:56,644
set of parentheses. The argument, 3, goes in that last set. It

28
00:01:56,744 --> 00:02:01,574
runs once, immediately, and its variables don't leak into the global scope.

29
00:02:01,966 --> 00:02:05,034
Both versions print 2, 4, 6.

30
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:08,728
Exam trap. If you leave off the parentheses

31
00:02:08,828 --> 00:02:11,673
around the arrow function, it's a syntax error.

32
00:02:14,766 --> 00:02:18,002
The classic function keyword version works the same way.

33
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:20,736
And here's the arrow version on a single line.

34
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:23,739
The async I I F E is worth memorizing. await is

35
00:02:23,839 --> 00:02:27,513
only allowed inside an async function, so you wrap the code

36
00:02:27,613 --> 00:02:31,415
in an async arrow function and call it immediately. That lets

37
00:02:31,515 --> 00:02:34,677
you write await fetch at the top level of a script.

38
00:02:35,066 --> 00:02:38,158
The first two each print a b three times.

39
00:02:41,233 --> 00:02:45,214
Practice question 1. Given a volume function of length, width, and

40
00:02:45,314 --> 00:02:48,987
height, write volumeA so you call it with three separate sets

41
00:02:49,087 --> 00:02:52,574
of parentheses, and volumeB so you call it with length and

42
00:02:52,674 --> 00:02:56,037
width together, then height. Pause the video and try it.

43
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:10,364
volumeA has three levels, so three arrows: l arrow w arrow h arrow the

44
00:03:10,464 --> 00:03:17,196
product. volumeB has two levels: the outer function takes l and w, and returns

45
00:03:17,296 --> 00:03:21,225
a function of h. Both give 24 for 2, 3, and 4.

46
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:29,187
Practice question 2. Rewrite greet and its call as a single

47
00:03:29,287 --> 00:03:32,431
arrow function I I F E, with no name. Then, what

48
00:03:32,531 --> 00:03:36,216
does the bank closure print? Pause the video and try it.

49
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:49,043
Wrap an arrow that takes name in parentheses, and call it right away

50
00:03:49,143 --> 00:03:52,800
with Terp. For the bank: 10 plus 5 gives 15, a new bank

51
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:57,785
starting at 0 gives 0, and acct dot balance is undefined, because balance

52
00:03:57,885 --> 00:04:02,088
is a variable inside the closure, not a property of the object.

53
00:04:06,166 --> 00:04:10,173
Recap. Curried calls like f of a b, then c, mean f returns

54
00:04:10,273 --> 00:04:15,129
a function of c. Closures keep the outer variables alive, with a fresh

55
00:04:15,229 --> 00:04:18,669
copy for each outer call. An I I F E is a function

56
00:04:18,769 --> 00:04:23,272
wrapped in parentheses and called right away. And the async I I F

57
00:04:23,372 --> 00:04:25,962
E lets you use await at the top level.

